<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">Great Speeches</h1><p class="ref-lede">60 speeches that moved history, from Pericles to Reagan. Each gives the setting, the key passage with its source, what followed and what it led to. 38 carry their full text, and 5 more link to it at a free source.</p></header>
<section class="sp-era"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">The ancient world</h2><div class="sp-grid"><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/pericles-funeral-oration"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pericles.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">Winter 431–430 BCE</span><span class="sp-card-t">Pericles' Funeral Oration</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Our constitution does not copy the laws of neighbouring states; we are rather a pattern to others than imitators ourselves.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Pericles<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/socrates-defence"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/socrates.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">399 BCE</span><span class="sp-card-t">Socrates' Defence at His Trial</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;The unexamined life is not worth living.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Socrates<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/demosthenes-first-philippic"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demosthenes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">c. 351 BCE</span><span class="sp-card-t">Demosthenes' First Philippic</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;When, then, men of Athens, when will you do what you must? What are you waiting for?&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Demosthenes<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/alexander-at-opis"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">324 BCE</span><span class="sp-card-t">Alexander's Speech at Opis</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Philip found you vagabonds and destitute of means, most of you clad in hides, feeding a few sheep up the mountain sides.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Alexander the Great<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/hannibal-before-the-ticinus"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hannibal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">Autumn 218 BCE</span><span class="sp-card-t">Hannibal to His Army before the Ticinus</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Here, soldiers, you must conquer or die.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Hannibal<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/cicero-against-catiline"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cicero.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">8 November 63 BCE</span><span class="sp-card-t">Cicero against Catiline</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;How long, Catiline, will you abuse our patience?&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Cicero<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/boudica-before-the-battle"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boudica.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">60 or 61 CE</span><span class="sp-card-t">Boudica before the Final Battle</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;This is a woman's resolve; as for men, they may live and be slaves.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Boudica<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/calgacus-at-mons-graupius"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/calgacus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">83 or 84 CE</span><span class="sp-card-t">Calgacus to the Caledonians</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;To robbery, slaughter, plunder, they give the lying name of empire; they make a solitude and call it peace.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Calgacus<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a></div></section>
<section class="sp-era"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">The medieval world</h2><div class="sp-grid"><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/muhammad-farewell-sermon"><span class="sp-card-k">March 632</span><span class="sp-card-t">Muhammad's Farewell Sermon</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Your Lord is one and your father is one. An Arab has no superiority over a non-Arab, nor a non-Arab over an Arab... except by piety.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Muhammad</span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/tariq-ibn-ziyad-the-sea-behind-you"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tariq-ibn-ziyad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">711</span><span class="sp-card-t">Tariq ibn Ziyad: The Sea Behind You</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;O people, where can you flee? The sea is behind you and the enemy before you.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Tariq ibn Ziyad</span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/urban-ii-at-clermont"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">27 November 1095</span><span class="sp-card-t">Pope Urban II at Clermont</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;It is the will of God! It is the will of God!&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Pope Urban II<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/genghis-khan-at-bukhara"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">1220</span><span class="sp-card-t">Genghis Khan at Bukhara</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;I am the punishment of God. If you had not committed great sins, God would not have sent a punishment like me upon you.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Genghis Khan</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/john-ball-at-blackheath"><span class="sp-card-k">June 1381</span><span class="sp-card-t">John Ball at Blackheath</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;When Adam delved and Eve span, who was then the gentleman?&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">John Ball</span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/joan-of-arc-before-her-judges"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">24 February 1431</span><span class="sp-card-t">Joan of Arc before Her Judges</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;If I am not, may God put me there; and if I am, may God so keep me.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Joan of Arc</span></a></div></section>
<section class="sp-era"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">The early modern world</h2><div class="sp-grid"><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/moctezuma-greets-cortes"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">8 November 1519</span><span class="sp-card-t">Moctezuma Greets Cortés</span><span class="sp-card-q">In substance: Our ancestors came to this land from far away, led by a lord who later departed, and we have always believed that his descendants would one day return to rule us. Since you come from where the sun rises and speak of a great lord who sent you, we take him to be our natural lord, and we will obey you in his place.</span><span class="sp-card-by">Moctezuma II<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/luther-at-worms"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">18 April 1521</span><span class="sp-card-t">Luther at the Diet of Worms</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Unless I am convinced by the testimony of Scripture or by clear reason... I cannot and will not recant anything, for to go against conscience is neither safe nor right.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Martin Luther<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/elizabeth-i-at-tilbury"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">August 1588</span><span class="sp-card-t">Elizabeth I at Tilbury</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;I know I have the body but of a weak and feeble woman; but I have the heart and stomach of a king, and of a king of England too.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Elizabeth I<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/charles-i-on-the-scaffold"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-england.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">30 January 1649</span><span class="sp-card-t">Charles I on the Scaffold</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;I go from a corruptible to an incorruptible Crown, where no disturbance can be.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Charles I</span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/cromwell-dismisses-the-rump"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">20 April 1653</span><span class="sp-card-t">Cromwell Dismisses the Rump Parliament</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;You have sat too long here for any good you have been doing. Depart, I say, and let us have done with you. In the name of God, go!&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Oliver Cromwell</span></a></div></section>
<section class="sp-era"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">The age of revolutions</h2><div class="sp-grid"><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/patrick-henry-liberty-or-death"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/patrick-henry.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">23 March 1775</span><span class="sp-card-t">Give Me Liberty or Give Me Death</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;I know not what course others may take; but as for me, give me liberty or give me death!&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Patrick Henry<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/wilberforce-against-the-slave-trade"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">12 May 1789</span><span class="sp-card-t">Wilberforce against the Slave Trade</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;So enormous, so dreadful, so irremediable did its wickedness appear that my own mind was completely made up for the abolition.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">William Wilberforce<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/danton-de-laudace"><span class="sp-card-k">2 September 1792</span><span class="sp-card-t">Danton: Audacity, Always Audacity</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Audacity, more audacity, always audacity, and France will be saved.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Georges Danton<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/toussaint-louverture-at-camp-turel"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">29 August 1793</span><span class="sp-card-t">Toussaint Louverture at Camp Turel</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;I want Liberty and Equality to reign in Saint-Domingue. I am working to make that happen. Unite yourselves to us, brothers, and fight with us for the same cause.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Toussaint Louverture</span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/robespierre-on-virtue-and-terror"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maximilien-robespierre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">5 February 1794</span><span class="sp-card-t">Robespierre on Virtue and Terror</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;If the mainspring of popular government in peacetime is virtue, the mainspring of popular government in revolution is at once virtue and terror: virtue, without which terror is fatal; terror, without which virtue is powerless.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Maximilien Robespierre<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/napoleon-to-the-army-of-italy"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">March 1796</span><span class="sp-card-t">Napoleon to the Army of Italy</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Soldiers, you are naked and ill fed... I will lead you into the most fertile plains in the world.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Napoleon<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/tecumseh-to-the-osages"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tecumseh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">1811</span><span class="sp-card-t">Tecumseh to the Osages</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Brothers, we all belong to one family; we are all children of the Great Spirit.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Tecumseh<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/bolivar-at-angostura"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">15 February 1819</span><span class="sp-card-t">Bolívar at Angostura</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Unity, unity, unity must be our motto.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Simón Bolívar<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a></div></section>
<section class="sp-era"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">The nineteenth century</h2><div class="sp-grid"><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/garibaldi-leaves-rome"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">2 July 1849</span><span class="sp-card-t">Garibaldi Leaves Rome</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;I offer neither pay, nor quarters, nor food; I offer only hunger, thirst, forced marches, battles and death. Let him who loves his country with his heart, and not merely with his lips, follow me.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Giuseppe Garibaldi</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/sojourner-truth-aint-i-a-woman"><span class="sp-card-k">May 1851</span><span class="sp-card-t">Sojourner Truth: Ain't I a Woman?</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;And ain't I a woman?&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Sojourner Truth<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/douglass-fourth-of-july"><span class="sp-card-k">5 July 1852</span><span class="sp-card-t">What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;What, to the American slave, is your Fourth of July?&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Frederick Douglass<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/bismarck-iron-and-blood"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">30 September 1862</span><span class="sp-card-t">Bismarck: Iron and Blood</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Not through speeches and majority decisions are the great questions of the day decided — that was the great mistake of 1848 and 1849 — but by iron and blood.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Otto von Bismarck</span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/lincoln-gettysburg-address"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">19 November 1863</span><span class="sp-card-t">The Gettysburg Address</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;...that government of the people, by the people, for the people, shall not perish from the earth.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Abraham Lincoln<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/lincoln-second-inaugural"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">4 March 1865</span><span class="sp-card-t">Lincoln's Second Inaugural Address</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;With malice toward none, with charity for all...&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Abraham Lincoln<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/susan-b-anthony-on-suffrage"><span class="sp-card-k">1873</span><span class="sp-card-t">Susan B. Anthony on Women's Right to Vote</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;It was we, the people; not we, the white male citizens; nor yet we, the male citizens; but we, the whole people, who formed the Union.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Susan B. Anthony<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/chief-joseph-surrender"><span class="sp-card-k">5 October 1877</span><span class="sp-card-t">Chief Joseph: I Will Fight No More Forever</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Hear me, my chiefs! I am tired; my heart is sick and sad. From where the sun now stands I will fight no more forever.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Chief Joseph<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/vivekananda-at-chicago"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">11 September 1893</span><span class="sp-card-t">Vivekananda at the Parliament of Religions</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Sisters and Brothers of America, it fills my heart with joy unspeakable to rise in response to the warm and cordial welcome which you have given us.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Swami Vivekananda<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a></div></section>
<section class="sp-era"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">The early twentieth century</h2><div class="sp-grid"><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/cixi-reform-edict"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">29 January 1901</span><span class="sp-card-t">The Empress Dowager's Edict of Reform</span><span class="sp-card-q">In substance: The great moral principles never change, but the methods of government must change with the times; China must learn from the strengths of other nations to remedy its own weaknesses.</span><span class="sp-card-by">Empress Dowager Cixi</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/pankhurst-freedom-or-death"><span class="sp-card-k">13 November 1913</span><span class="sp-card-t">Emmeline Pankhurst: Freedom or Death</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;I am here as a soldier who has temporarily left the field of battle in order to explain... what civil war is like when civil war is waged by women.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Emmeline Pankhurst<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/wilson-fourteen-points"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">8 January 1918</span><span class="sp-card-t">Wilson's Fourteen Points</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Open covenants of peace, openly arrived at, after which there shall be no private international understandings of any kind...&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Woodrow Wilson<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/ataturk-nutuk"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">15–20 October 1927</span><span class="sp-card-t">Atatürk's Great Speech (Nutuk)</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Turkish youth! Your first duty is to preserve and defend Turkish independence and the Turkish Republic forever.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/fdr-nothing-to-fear"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">4 March 1933</span><span class="sp-card-t">Roosevelt: The Only Thing We Have to Fear</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;So, first of all, let me assert my firm belief that the only thing we have to fear is fear itself — nameless, unreasoning, unjustified terror which paralyzes needed efforts to convert retreat into advance.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Franklin D. Roosevelt<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/haile-selassie-at-the-league-of-nations"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haile-selassie.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">30 June 1936</span><span class="sp-card-t">Haile Selassie at the League of Nations</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;I, Haile Selassie I, Emperor of Ethiopia, am here today to claim that justice which is due to my people, and the assistance promised to it eight months ago...&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Haile Selassie<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/churchill-we-shall-fight"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winston-churchill.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">4 June 1940</span><span class="sp-card-t">Churchill: We Shall Fight on the Beaches</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;We shall fight on the beaches... we shall never surrender.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Winston Churchill<span class="sp-card-ft"> · complete text linked</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/de-gaulle-appeal-of-18-june"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">18 June 1940</span><span class="sp-card-t">De Gaulle's Appeal of 18 June</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Whatever happens, the flame of French resistance must not and shall not die.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Charles de Gaulle</span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/fdr-a-date-which-will-live-in-infamy"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">8 December 1941</span><span class="sp-card-t">Roosevelt: A Date Which Will Live in Infamy</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Yesterday, December 7, 1941 — a date which will live in infamy — the United States of America was suddenly and deliberately attacked by naval and air forces of the Empire of Japan.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Franklin D. Roosevelt<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/gandhi-quit-india"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">8 August 1942</span><span class="sp-card-t">Gandhi: Do or Die</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Here is a mantra, a short one, that I give you... The mantra is: 'Do or Die.'&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Mahatma Gandhi<span class="sp-card-ft"> · complete text linked</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/hirohito-surrender-broadcast"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hirohito.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">15 August 1945</span><span class="sp-card-t">Hirohito's Surrender Broadcast</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;...we have resolved to pave the way for a grand peace for all the generations to come by enduring the unendurable and suffering what is insufferable.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Hirohito<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/ho-chi-minh-declaration-of-independence"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">2 September 1945</span><span class="sp-card-t">Ho Chi Minh Declares Vietnam's Independence</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;All men are created equal. They are endowed by their Creator with certain inalienable rights; among these are Life, Liberty, and the pursuit of Happiness. This immortal statement was made in the Declaration of Independence of the United States of America in 1776.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Ho Chi Minh<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a></div></section>
<section class="sp-era"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">The world since 1945</h2><div class="sp-grid"><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/churchill-iron-curtain"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winston-churchill.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">5 March 1946</span><span class="sp-card-t">Churchill: An Iron Curtain Has Descended</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;From Stettin in the Baltic to Trieste in the Adriatic, an iron curtain has descended across the Continent.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Winston Churchill<span class="sp-card-ft"> · complete text linked</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/jinnah-to-the-constituent-assembly"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">11 August 1947</span><span class="sp-card-t">Jinnah to Pakistan's Constituent Assembly</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;You are free; you are free to go to your temples, you are free to go to your mosques or to any other place of worship in this State of Pakistan. You may belong to any religion or caste or creed — that has nothing to do with the business of the State.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/nehru-tryst-with-destiny"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">14 August 1947</span><span class="sp-card-t">Nehru: Tryst with Destiny</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;At the stroke of the midnight hour, when the world sleeps, India will awake to life and freedom.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Jawaharlal Nehru<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/mao-the-chinese-people-have-stood-up"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mao-zedong.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">21 September 1949</span><span class="sp-card-t">Mao: The Chinese People Have Stood Up</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;The Chinese people, comprising one quarter of humanity, have now stood up.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Mao Zedong</span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/nasser-nationalises-the-suez-canal"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">26 July 1956</span><span class="sp-card-t">Nasser Nationalises the Suez Canal</span><span class="sp-card-q">In substance: The Suez Canal Company is nationalised. Its revenues, which have gone to foreign shareholders, will now pay for the High Dam, and the canal will be run by Egyptians.</span><span class="sp-card-by">Gamal Abdel Nasser</span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/nkrumah-ghana-is-free"><span class="sp-card-k">6 March 1957</span><span class="sp-card-t">Nkrumah: Ghana Is Free Forever</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Our independence is meaningless unless it is linked up with the total liberation of the African continent.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Kwame Nkrumah</span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/lumumba-independence-day-speech"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">30 June 1960</span><span class="sp-card-t">Lumumba's Independence Day Speech</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;We have known sarcasm, insults and blows that we had to endure morning, noon and night, because we were Black.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Patrice Lumumba</span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/eisenhower-farewell-address"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">17 January 1961</span><span class="sp-card-t">Eisenhower's Farewell Address</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;In the councils of government, we must guard against the acquisition of unwarranted influence, whether sought or unsought, by the military-industrial complex.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Dwight D. Eisenhower<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card" href="/speeches/kennedy-ask-not"><span class="sp-card-k">20 January 1961</span><span class="sp-card-t">Kennedy: Ask Not What Your Country Can Do for You</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;And so, my fellow Americans: ask not what your country can do for you — ask what you can do for your country.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">John F. Kennedy<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/mlk-i-have-a-dream"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">28 August 1963</span><span class="sp-card-t">Martin Luther King Jr.: I Have a Dream</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;...not be judged by the color of their skin but by the content of their character.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Martin Luther King Jr.<span class="sp-card-ft"> · complete text linked</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/mandela-statement-from-the-dock"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">20 April 1964</span><span class="sp-card-t">Mandela's Statement from the Dock</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;It is an ideal for which I am prepared to die.&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Nelson Mandela<span class="sp-card-ft"> · complete text linked</span></span></a><a class="sp-card has-pic" href="/speeches/reagan-tear-down-this-wall"><span class="sp-card-pic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"></span><span class="sp-card-k">12 June 1987</span><span class="sp-card-t">Reagan: Tear Down This Wall</span><span class="sp-card-q">&ldquo;Mr. Gorbachev, open this gate! Mr. Gorbachev, tear down this wall!&rdquo;</span><span class="sp-card-by">Ronald Reagan<span class="sp-card-ft"> · full text</span></span></a></div></section>
